// Domain 1: Detection (16%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d1-01", d: 1,
  q: "A company has 60 AWS accounts in an organization in AWS Organizations. The company's security team must enable Amazon GuardDuty in every account, including accounts that are created later. The security team must manage findings from a dedicated security tooling account instead of the management account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Designate the security tooling account as the GuardDuty delegated administrator. Configure GuardDuty to enable automatically for all accounts in the organization.",
      "Enable GuardDuty in each account by using an AWS CloudFormation StackSet. Send findings from every account to an Amazon S3 bucket in the security tooling account.",
      "Enable GuardDuty in the management account. Invite each member account from the management account, and accept each invitation in the member account.",
      "Create an AWS Lambda function in each account that enables GuardDuty. Run the function from an Amazon EventBridge rule when a new account is created."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A GuardDuty delegated administrator manages GuardDuty for the whole organization, and the auto-enable setting turns on GuardDuty for existing and new member accounts. Findings from all members appear in the delegated administrator account, so the management account is not used for daily work.",
      "StackSets can enable GuardDuty, but findings in an S3 bucket are not a managed multi-account view, and the company must keep the deployment and the bucket working.",
      "Invitations are the legacy method for accounts outside an organization. Each new account would need a manual invitation, and the management account would hold the findings.",
      "This is custom code that the company must build and maintain for a feature that GuardDuty provides through its organization settings."] },

{ id: "d1-02", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses AWS Security Hub CSPM in four AWS Regions for all accounts in its organization. The company's security analysts must review findings from all accounts and all four Regions in one place, from the delegated administrator account.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Choose a home Region in the delegated administrator account. Link the other three Regions to the home Region for cross-Region aggregation.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule in each Region that sends new findings to an Amazon SQS queue in one Region for the analysts to read each day.",
      "Create an AWS Config aggregator in the delegated administrator account. Review the Security Hub CSPM findings in the AWS Config console.",
      "Export findings from each Region to Amazon S3 every day. Query the exported findings by using Amazon Athena from a single Region."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Cross-Region aggregation sends findings, insights, and control status from linked Regions to a home Region. The delegated administrator then sees findings from every member account and every linked Region in one place, and updates sync back to the linked Regions.",
      "A queue gives analysts raw messages without the Security Hub CSPM console, workflow status, or filters, and the company must maintain rules in every Region.",
      "An AWS Config aggregator collects configuration and compliance data. It does not show Security Hub CSPM findings.",
      "Daily exports are delayed and lose the managed workflow of Security Hub CSPM, and the company must build and run the export process."] },

{ id: "d1-03", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security team must receive an email within minutes of any Amazon GuardDuty finding that has a severity of 7.0 or higher in the company's AWS account. Lower-severity findings must not generate email.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches GuardDuty findings with a numeric severity of 7 or higher. Target an Amazon SNS topic with an email subscription.",
      "Create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm on a GuardDuty finding count metric. Set the alarm threshold to 7, and notify an Amazon SNS topic with an email subscription.",
      "Export GuardDuty findings to an Amazon S3 bucket. Run an Amazon Athena query every hour, and email the query results for the findings that have a high severity.",
      "Turn on finding notifications in the GuardDuty console settings. Set the notification frequency to 15 minutes for findings of every severity level."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["GuardDuty sends each finding to EventBridge as an event. A rule with a numeric matching pattern on detail.severity selects only high-severity findings, and an SNS topic delivers the email within minutes.",
      "A count threshold of 7 alerts on the number of findings, not on their severity, so it cannot separate high-severity findings from low-severity ones.",
      "An hourly query adds delay and custom work, and it does not meet the requirement to alert within minutes.",
      "The frequency setting controls how often updated findings are exported. It does not send email, and it does not filter by severity."] },

{ id: "d1-04", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security policy requires an alert whenever any IAM user signs in to the AWS Management Console without MFA. The company already sends AWS CloudTrail events for the account to an Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create a metric filter on the log group for successful ConsoleLogin events where MFAUsed is not Yes. Create an alarm on the metric that notifies an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Create an IAM Access Analyzer unused access analyzer. Send each analyzer finding about IAM users that do not use MFA to an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Enable the iam-user-mfa-enabled AWS Config managed rule. Send each compliance change for the rule to an Amazon SNS topic for the security team.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule for findings about console sign-ins, and target an Amazon SNS topic for the team."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ConsoleLogin events in CloudTrail record whether MFA was used. A metric filter on these events turns each sign-in without MFA into a metric data point, and an alarm on the metric sends the alert. AWS security standards recommend this control.",
      "Access Analyzer finds unused roles, keys, and permissions. It does not report individual sign-ins without MFA.",
      "This rule reports whether a user has an MFA device. A user with a device can still sign in without using it, and the rule does not report sign-in events.",
      "GuardDuty reports suspicious behavior, not every sign-in that skips MFA, so it does not meet the policy."] },

{ id: "d1-05", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores data in thousands of Amazon S3 buckets across 40 accounts in AWS Organizations. The company's security team must continually identify which buckets contain sensitive data, such as financial and personal information, and must review the results from one account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Designate a delegated administrator account for Amazon Macie. Enable Macie for all member accounts, and turn on automated sensitive data discovery.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty S3 Protection in all accounts. Review the S3 findings in the GuardDuty delegated administrator account for the organization.",
      "Create an Amazon Athena query that scans every bucket for sensitive data patterns. Schedule the query to run each week from a single account.",
      "Enable Amazon Inspector in all accounts. Review the findings for S3 buckets from the Inspector delegated administrator account for the organization."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Macie discovers sensitive data in S3 by using machine learning and pattern matching. Automated sensitive data discovery samples objects across buckets continually, and the delegated administrator sees the results for all member accounts.",
      "GuardDuty S3 Protection detects suspicious access to S3 data. It does not classify the data in the buckets.",
      "Athena cannot query arbitrary objects without tables and formats, and the company would have to build and maintain the detection patterns.",
      "Amazon Inspector scans compute workloads for vulnerabilities. It does not scan S3 buckets for sensitive data."] },

{ id: "d1-06", d: 1,
  q: "A company must centralize security logs from all accounts and Regions in its organization, including AWS CloudTrail management events, VPC Flow Logs, and Amazon Route 53 Resolver query logs. The logs must be stored in the company's own Amazon S3 buckets in an open, standard schema that several analytics tools can query.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Enable Amazon Security Lake from a delegated administrator account. Add the AWS log sources for all accounts and Regions in the organization.",
      "Create an organization trail and VPC flow logs in each account. Send all logs to one bucket, and define the AWS Glue tables for each source manually.",
      "Enable AWS Security Hub CSPM in all accounts and Regions. Export the findings to an Amazon S3 bucket in a standard format every day.",
      "Send all logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs in each account. Create subscription filters that stream the logs to an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Security Lake collects security data from AWS sources such as CloudTrail management events, VPC Flow Logs, and Route 53 Resolver query logs into S3 buckets in the company's account. It normalizes the data to the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF), so many tools can query it.",
      "This collects the raw logs, but each source keeps its own format, and the company must build and maintain the tables and the collection for every account.",
      "Security Hub CSPM findings are not the raw logs, so the logs themselves would not be centralized.",
      "OpenSearch can search the logs, but the data is not stored in the company's S3 buckets in an open schema, and the company must manage the subscriptions in every account."] },

{ id: "d1-07", d: 1,
  q: "A company must evaluate every account in its organization against the same set of AWS Config rules, which the security team packages together with remediation actions. Accounts that join the organization later must be evaluated automatically. The security team manages AWS Config from a delegated administrator account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Deploy the rules and remediation actions as an organization conformance pack from the delegated administrator account.",
      "Create the rules in the delegated administrator account. Use an AWS Config aggregator to apply the rules to every member account.",
      "Deploy the rules to each existing account by using AWS CloudFormation stacks. Repeat the stack deployment for each new account.",
      "Add the rules to an SCP that is attached to the organization root. Review the compliance results in the AWS Organizations console."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A conformance pack is a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions that is deployed as one unit. An organization conformance pack deploys it to all member accounts, including accounts that join later, and it can be managed from a delegated administrator account.",
      "An aggregator collects compliance data from other accounts. It does not deploy rules to them.",
      "Separate stacks work, but each new account needs another deployment, which the company must remember and run.",
      "SCPs limit which actions principals can perform. They cannot hold or evaluate AWS Config rules."] },

{ id: "d1-08", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs 800 Amazon EC2 instances that are managed by AWS Systems Manager. A host-based intrusion detection agent must be installed and running on every instance at all times. The company's security team has found that engineers sometimes stop the agent and forget to start it again.\n\nWhich solution will keep the agent in the required state with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create a Systems Manager State Manager association that installs and starts the agent. Run the association on a schedule against all managed instances.",
      "Run a Systems Manager Run Command document once to install the agent on all instances. Repeat the command whenever engineers report problems with the agent.",
      "Create an AWS Config custom rule that checks for the agent process. Ask the engineers to restart the agent when the rule reports noncompliance.",
      "Add the agent to the AMI that the instances use. Replace any instance that has a stopped agent during the next scheduled deployment."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A State Manager association defines the state that managed instances must keep and applies it on a schedule. If an engineer stops the agent, the next run of the association starts it again without manual work.",
      "Run Command runs once. It does not keep the agent running after engineers stop it.",
      "The rule detects the problem but leaves the fix to people, so the agent can stay stopped for a long time.",
      "Baking the agent into the AMI installs it, but nothing restarts it after it is stopped, and replacements wait for the next deployment."] },

{ id: "d1-09", d: 1,
  q: "A company must continuously check its AWS accounts against AWS security best practices, such as whether Amazon S3 buckets block public access and whether Amazon EBS snapshots are public. The results must include a security score for each account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Enable AWS Security Hub CSPM in each account. Enable the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard.",
      "Run the AWS Trusted Advisor security checks in each account every week. Export the results to a spreadsheet to calculate a score.",
      "Enable Amazon Inspector in each account. Review the vulnerability risk score for each account in the Amazon Inspector console.",
      "Write AWS Config custom rules for each best practice by using AWS Lambda. Calculate a score from the rule results in a dashboard."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard is a set of controls that continuously evaluate accounts and resources against AWS security best practices. Security Hub CSPM calculates a security score for each enabled standard from the control results.",
      "Weekly manual exports are not continuous, and the score would have to be calculated by hand.",
      "Amazon Inspector scores software vulnerabilities on compute resources. It does not check configurations such as public snapshots.",
      "Custom rules and a custom score require code for each practice, which AWS already provides as managed controls."] },

{ id: "d1-10", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs containerized workloads on Amazon EKS clusters. The company's security team must detect threats inside running containers, such as a process that connects to a cryptocurrency mining pool or an unexpected shell. The company already uses Amazon GuardDuty.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Enable GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring for Amazon EKS. Let GuardDuty manage the security agent on the clusters automatically.",
      "Enable GuardDuty EKS Audit Log Monitoring only. Review the findings that GuardDuty generates from the Kubernetes audit logs.",
      "Enable Amazon Inspector scanning for the container images in Amazon ECR. Review the findings for packages with known vulnerabilities.",
      "Enable VPC Flow Logs for the cluster subnets. Search the flow logs for connections to the IP addresses of known mining pools."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Runtime Monitoring uses a security agent on the nodes to watch operating system events such as process execution and network connections inside containers. GuardDuty can deploy and update the agent automatically for EKS clusters, and it generates findings such as cryptocurrency mining and unexpected shells.",
      "Audit log monitoring analyzes Kubernetes API activity. It does not see processes or connections inside running containers.",
      "Image scanning finds known vulnerabilities before and after deployment. It does not detect what a running container does.",
      "Flow logs show IP traffic, but the team would have to maintain lists of mining pool addresses, and flow logs cannot see processes or shells."] },

{ id: "d1-11", d: 1,
  q: "A company wants to detect unusual spikes in the rate of write management API calls in its AWS account, such as a sudden increase in RunInstances or DeleteBucket calls, without defining a threshold for each API. An AWS CloudTrail trail already logs all management events.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Enable CloudTrail Insights on the trail for the API call rate. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that sends Insights events to an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Create an Amazon CloudWatch metric filter for each write API in the trail's log group. Create a static threshold alarm for each new metric that the filters create.",
      "Enable AWS Config recording for all resource types. Create an AWS Config custom rule that counts the configuration changes every hour.",
      "Enable Amazon Macie for the trail's S3 bucket. Create an EventBridge rule for Macie findings about the log files that the trail delivers."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail Insights learns the normal rate of write management API calls and records an Insights event when the rate is unusual. No per-API threshold is needed, and an EventBridge rule can send each Insights event to the team.",
      "This needs a threshold for every API, which the company does not want to define.",
      "A custom rule requires code, and counting configuration changes does not measure API call rates.",
      "Macie classifies data in S3. It does not analyze the rate of API calls in the log files."] },

{ id: "d1-12", d: 1,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty generates an UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/InstanceCredentialExfiltration.OutsideAWS finding for an IAM role that is attached to an Amazon EC2 instance.\n\nWhat does this finding indicate?",
  o: ["Temporary credentials that were created for the instance's role are being used from an IP address outside AWS.",
      "The instance is communicating with an IP address that is on a threat list of known command and control servers.",
      "An IAM user's long-term access key is being used from a country where the account has never been used before.",
      "The instance metadata service on the instance allows IMDSv1 requests that do not use a session token."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["This finding means that credentials that were issued to an EC2 instance through its role are being used from an external IP address. That usually means the credentials were stolen from the instance, so the role's sessions should be revoked and the instance investigated.",
      "Communication with a command and control server produces a Backdoor finding type, not a credential exfiltration finding.",
      "The finding is about instance role credentials, which are temporary, not an IAM user's long-term access key.",
      "GuardDuty does not report the IMDS version as this finding. IMDSv2 reduces the risk of credential theft, but it is a separate setting."] },

{ id: "d1-13", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses AWS Security Hub CSPM with a delegated administrator account. The on-call team must see findings from 10 production accounts first. These findings must be set to CRITICAL severity automatically when they are created, and findings from other accounts must keep their original severity.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create a Security Hub CSPM automation rule in the delegated administrator account. Match the production account IDs, and set the severity label to CRITICAL.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule for new findings. Invoke an AWS Lambda function that updates the severity label of each new finding from a production account.",
      "Create a custom action in Security Hub CSPM. Ask the on-call team to run the custom action on each new finding that comes from a production account.",
      "Create a Security Hub CSPM insight that filters findings by the production account IDs. Sort the insight results by severity in the console."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automation rules update findings automatically when they are created or updated, based on criteria such as the account ID. The delegated administrator can create a rule that sets the severity label for findings from the production accounts without custom code.",
      "A Lambda function can do this, but it is custom code that the company must maintain for a built-in feature.",
      "A custom action needs a person to run it for each finding, which is neither automatic nor fast.",
      "An insight groups and sorts findings for viewing. It does not change their severity."] },

{ id: "d1-14", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores sensitive reports in Amazon S3. The company's security team must be alerted when an IAM principal accesses objects in an unusual way, such as calls from a known malicious IP address or an abnormal volume of GetObject requests. The team does not want to manage AWS CloudTrail data events or custom rules.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable Amazon GuardDuty with S3 Protection. Send the GuardDuty findings about S3 data access to the security team through Amazon EventBridge.",
      "Enable Amazon Macie with automated sensitive data discovery. Send the Macie findings about the buckets to the team through Amazon EventBridge.",
      "Enable S3 server access logging for the buckets. Query the access logs by using Amazon Athena each week to look for unusual activity.",
      "Enable AWS Config recording for S3 buckets. Create a rule that reports when an object is read from an IP address on a known malicious list."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 Protection lets GuardDuty monitor S3 data events and generate findings for suspicious or anomalous access, such as access from malicious IP addresses or unusual request volumes. GuardDuty analyzes the data events itself, so the team does not manage a trail for them.",
      "Macie finds sensitive data in buckets. It does not detect unusual access patterns by principals.",
      "Weekly queries are slow and require the team to build its own detection logic.",
      "AWS Config records configuration changes, not object reads, and the rule would need custom logic."] },

{ id: "d1-15", d: 1,
  q: "A company has 50 accounts in AWS Organizations. The company's security team must automatically scan all existing and new Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon ECR container images, and AWS Lambda functions for software vulnerabilities in every account. The team must view all findings in one account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Designate a delegated administrator for Amazon Inspector. Activate scanning for all member accounts, and turn on automatic activation for new accounts.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty Malware Protection in each account. Review the malware findings in the GuardDuty delegated administrator account.",
      "Install a third-party vulnerability scanner on each EC2 instance. Send the scanner results to an Amazon S3 bucket in a single account.",
      "Enable AWS Config in each account. Use an AWS Config aggregator to collect the installed software inventory from every member account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Amazon Inspector continually scans EC2 instances, ECR images, and Lambda functions for software vulnerabilities and network exposure. A delegated administrator can activate scanning for member accounts and new accounts automatically, and it sees all findings in one place.",
      "Malware Protection looks for malware on volumes. It does not assess known software vulnerabilities, and it does not cover container images in ECR or Lambda functions.",
      "A self-managed scanner on EC2 does not cover ECR images or Lambda functions, and the company must install and maintain it everywhere.",
      "A software inventory lists packages but does not match them against vulnerability data."] },

{ id: "d1-16", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses AWS Organizations and has a dedicated log archive account. The company must record management events from all current and future accounts in every AWS Region. The logs must be encrypted with a customer managed AWS KMS key, and auditors must be able to verify that log files were not modified after delivery.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an organization trail that applies to all Regions. Deliver the trail to a bucket in the log archive account, with SSE-KMS encryption and log file validation.",
      "Create a trail in each account by using a CloudFormation StackSet. Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket in the log archive account to protect the log files.",
      "Create an AWS CloudTrail Lake event data store in each account. Grant the auditors read access to the event data store in each account.",
      "Enable AWS Config in all accounts and Regions. Deliver the configuration history to the log archive account bucket with SSE-KMS encryption."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An organization trail logs events for every account in the organization, including new accounts, in all Regions. Delivering it to the log archive account with SSE-KMS meets the encryption requirement, and log file integrity validation creates signed digest files that prove whether log files changed.",
      "Separate trails must be added for each new account, and versioning keeps old versions but does not prove that a file was not modified.",
      "Separate event data stores per account do not create one archive, and they do not deliver log files to the log archive account.",
      "Configuration history records resource changes, not management API events, so it does not replace CloudTrail."] },

{ id: "d1-17", d: 1,
  q: "A company has an AWS CloudTrail trail that logs management events. The company must also log every object-level read and write in one Amazon S3 bucket that stores payroll files. CloudTrail costs must stay low, so object activity in the company's other buckets must not be logged.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add S3 data events to the trail. Use an advanced event selector that matches resources.ARN values that start with the payroll bucket's ARN.",
      "Enable S3 server access logging for every bucket in the account. Delete the access logs for every bucket other than the payroll bucket each day.",
      "Add S3 data events for all current and future buckets to the trail. Filter the payroll bucket's events later by using Amazon Athena.",
      "Enable CloudTrail Insights on the trail. Configure Insights to record the object reads and writes for the payroll bucket only."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Data events record object-level API activity such as GetObject and PutObject. Advanced event selectors can filter data events on fields such as resources.ARN, so the trail logs and charges for only the payroll bucket's object activity.",
      "Server access logs are a different log source without full IAM identity details, and logging every bucket before deleting logs adds cost and work.",
      "Logging data events for every bucket is the costly result that the company wants to avoid.",
      "Insights detects unusual rates of management API activity. It does not record individual object reads and writes."] },

{ id: "d1-18", d: 1,
  q: "A company's auditors need to run SQL queries over 7 years of AWS CloudTrail management events from all accounts in the company's organization. The company does not want to build or maintain an extract, transform, and load (ETL) pipeline or manage table partitions.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an organization event data store in AWS CloudTrail Lake with a 7-year retention period. Run the auditors' SQL queries in CloudTrail Lake.",
      "Deliver an organization trail to Amazon S3. Create Amazon Athena tables for the logs, and add a new partition for every account, Region, and day.",
      "Create a CloudTrail trail in each account that delivers to Amazon S3. Load the files into a relational database each month for SQL queries.",
      "Stream the events to an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain. Keep 7 years of indexes in the domain, and query them by using OpenSearch."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail Lake stores events in managed event data stores that can be queried with SQL, without an ETL pipeline or partitions to manage. An organization event data store collects events from all accounts, and the retention period can be set to 7 years.",
      "Athena works, but adding partitions for every account, Region, and day is the table maintenance that the company wants to avoid.",
      "A monthly load into a database is an ETL pipeline that the company would have to build and run.",
      "Seven years of indexes in OpenSearch is costly to run, and the company would manage the domain and the ingestion."] },

{ id: "d1-19", d: 1,
  q: "A company's applications write logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs in 30 workload accounts. The company's security team must keep a copy of these logs in a dedicated logging account within minutes of each event. The copies must remain available even if an attacker deletes log groups in a workload account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a CloudWatch Logs destination in the logging account that delivers to Amazon Data Firehose. Add a subscription filter in each workload account.",
      "Set up CloudWatch cross-account observability with the logging account as the monitoring account. View the workload logs from the logging account in the console.",
      "Set the retention period of every log group in the workload accounts to Never expire. Grant the security team read access to those log groups.",
      "Export each log group to Amazon S3 in the workload account every day. Replicate the export bucket to an S3 bucket in the logging account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A cross-account subscription sends log events in near real time from each workload account to a destination in the logging account, which stores them, for example in Amazon S3 through Firehose. The copies live in the logging account, so deleting a source log group does not remove them.",
      "Cross-account observability lets the logging account view the source data, but it does not copy it, so deleted log groups are gone.",
      "Longer retention keeps the logs only in the workload accounts, where an attacker can still delete them.",
      "Daily exports do not deliver copies within minutes, and the export bucket in the workload account is also exposed to the attacker."] },

{ id: "d1-20", d: 1,
  q: "A company must collect the /var/log/secure file from 200 Amazon Linux instances into Amazon CloudWatch Logs to investigate failed SSH sign-in attempts. The instances are managed by AWS Systems Manager.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Store a CloudWatch agent configuration that collects /var/log/secure in Parameter Store. Use Systems Manager to install the agent and apply the configuration.",
      "Enable VPC Flow Logs for the instance subnets. Send the flow logs to CloudWatch Logs, and search the flow logs for failed SSH sign-in attempts.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for the instances. Search the trail for SSH sign-in events from each of the 200 instances.",
      "Create a cron job on each instance that copies /var/log/secure to Amazon S3 every hour. Query the copied files by using Amazon Athena."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The CloudWatch agent collects log files from instances and sends them to CloudWatch Logs. Systems Manager can install the agent on every managed instance and apply one shared configuration from Parameter Store.",
      "Flow logs record IP traffic metadata. They cannot show whether an SSH sign-in succeeded or failed.",
      "CloudTrail records AWS API calls. Operating system sign-ins on an instance are not API calls.",
      "Cron jobs on each instance are custom work to maintain, and the files would not be in CloudWatch Logs."] },

{ id: "d1-21", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security team must be able to investigate which Amazon EC2 instances in a VPC communicate with suspicious external IP addresses. The VPC has no flow logs. The team needs to run SQL queries over months of traffic data at the lowest cost.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a VPC flow log that publishes to Amazon S3 in Apache Parquet format. Query the flow logs by using Amazon Athena.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty for the account. Query the VPC Flow Logs that GuardDuty analyzes by using Amazon Athena.",
      "Enable Amazon Route 53 Resolver query logging for the VPC. Query the DNS logs for the external IP addresses by using Athena.",
      "Enable traffic mirroring on every instance. Store the mirrored packets in Amazon S3, and query the packets by using Athena."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Flow logs record the source and destination of IP traffic for network interfaces. Publishing to S3 in Parquet format keeps storage low, and Athena queries Parquet files faster and scans less data than plain text.",
      "GuardDuty analyzes its own independent stream of flow log data. That data is not available for the team to query.",
      "DNS query logs show domain names that were looked up, not the IP traffic between instances and external addresses.",
      "Packet capture is expensive and much more detailed than needed, and Athena is not a packet analysis tool."] },

{ id: "d1-22", d: 1,
  q: "A company connects 40 VPCs through AWS Transit Gateway. The company's security team must record metadata about all traffic that passes through the transit gateway between VPCs, including the source and destination attachments, from one place.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a transit gateway flow log for the transit gateway. Publish the flow log to Amazon CloudWatch Logs or Amazon S3.",
      "Create a VPC flow log for every subnet in all 40 VPCs. Combine the logs in one bucket to rebuild the traffic between the VPCs.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty in all accounts. Export the GuardDuty findings about the transit gateway to Amazon S3.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for the transit gateway. Query the events that describe each connection."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Transit gateway flow logs capture information about IP traffic that goes through a transit gateway, including the attachments that it passes between. One flow log on the transit gateway covers all of its traffic.",
      "Flow logs in every VPC are many logs to manage, and they do not show which transit gateway attachments the traffic used.",
      "GuardDuty findings describe threats, not a record of all traffic.",
      "CloudTrail records API calls to manage the transit gateway, not the network traffic that passes through it."] },

{ id: "d1-23", d: 1,
  q: "A company suspects that malware on some Amazon EC2 instances is resolving command and control domain names. The company's security team must record every DNS query that the instances in its VPCs send to the Amazon Route 53 Resolver, including the instance that sent each query.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Configure Route 53 Resolver query logging for the VPCs. Send the query logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, Amazon S3, or Amazon Data Firehose.",
      "Configure Route 53 public DNS query logging for the company's public hosted zones. Send the logs to CloudWatch Logs in us-east-1.",
      "Create VPC flow logs for the instance subnets. Filter the flow logs for UDP and TCP traffic on port 53 to see each domain name that was queried.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for Route 53. Search the trail for the DNS queries that each Amazon EC2 instance sends."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Resolver query logging records DNS queries that resources in the VPC send to the Resolver, including the domain name, the query type, and the source instance. It covers queries for any domain, including malicious ones.",
      "Public DNS query logging records queries that public resolvers send for the company's own hosted zones. It does not show what the instances look up.",
      "Flow logs show that traffic went to port 53, but they do not contain the domain names, and Resolver traffic is not captured in flow logs.",
      "CloudTrail does not record DNS queries from instances."] },

{ id: "d1-24", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses AWS WAF to protect an Application Load Balancer. The company's security team must keep a full record of every request that AWS WAF evaluates, including the rule that matched each request. The Authorization header must not appear in the logs.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable logging for the web ACL to an Amazon S3 bucket or Amazon CloudWatch Logs. Configure the logging configuration to redact the Authorization header.",
      "Enable access logs for the Application Load Balancer. Delete the Authorization header from each log file after it is delivered to Amazon S3.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for AWS WAF. Configure CloudTrail to exclude the request headers from each of the recorded events.",
      "Enable sampled requests for each rule in the web ACL. Review the sampled requests in the AWS WAF console at the end of each day."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS WAF logging records every request that the web ACL evaluates, including the terminating rule. The logging configuration can redact fields such as specific headers, so the Authorization value is never written to the logs.",
      "Load balancer access logs do not record which AWS WAF rule matched, and the header would be stored before anyone removed it.",
      "CloudTrail records API calls that manage AWS WAF, not the web requests that AWS WAF inspects.",
      "Sampled requests are a small sample of recent traffic, not a full record."] },

{ id: "d1-25", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs Amazon EKS clusters. The company's security team must keep a record of every request to the Kubernetes API server, including the user or role that made each request, for investigations.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Enable the audit log type in Amazon EKS control plane logging. Send the logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for the EKS clusters. Search the trail for each Kubernetes API request.",
      "Enable VPC Flow Logs for the cluster subnets. Search the logs for traffic to the Kubernetes API endpoint.",
      "Install the Amazon CloudWatch agent on the worker nodes. Collect the kubelet log files from each node."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["EKS control plane logging can send Kubernetes audit logs to CloudWatch Logs. The audit log records each request to the Kubernetes API server and the identity that made it.",
      "CloudTrail records calls to the Amazon EKS API, such as creating a cluster, not requests to the Kubernetes API server inside the cluster.",
      "Flow logs show network connections, not the API requests or the identity behind them.",
      "Kubelet logs on worker nodes do not record every request to the control plane's API server."] },

{ id: "d1-26", d: 1,
  q: "A company sends AWS CloudTrail events to an Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group. During an investigation, a security engineer must find every API call in the last 24 hours that failed with AccessDenied, grouped by the IAM principal that made it, within minutes.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Run a CloudWatch Logs Insights query on the log group that filters errorCode for AccessDenied and counts the results by userIdentity.arn.",
      "Export the log group to Amazon S3. Create an AWS Glue crawler and an Amazon Athena table for the export, and query the exported events.",
      "Create a metric filter for AccessDenied events on the log group. Wait for the metric to collect data, and review the metric in a dashboard.",
      "Open the CloudTrail Event history page in the console. Download the events one page at a time, and group them by principal in a spreadsheet."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudWatch Logs Insights queries log data in place, with filter and stats commands that can count matching events by any field, such as userIdentity.arn. Results return in seconds without moving data.",
      "An export, a crawler, and a table take time to set up, and the question needs an answer within minutes.",
      "A metric filter counts future events only. It cannot report past AccessDenied calls or group them by principal.",
      "Downloading events page by page and grouping them by hand is slow and error prone."] },

{ id: "d1-27", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores 3 years of AWS CloudTrail logs from an organization trail in Amazon S3. A security engineer must run ad hoc SQL queries over these logs. Queries must scan as little data as possible, and the engineer does not want to add partitions manually each day.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon Athena table for the logs with partition projection on account, Region, and date. Filter each query on those columns.",
      "Load all of the log files into an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain each night. Run the SQL queries from OpenSearch Dashboards instead.",
      "Create an Athena table without any partitions. Run every query against the full 3 years of log files that are stored in the bucket.",
      "Use Amazon Macie to classify all of the log files in the bucket. Run the SQL queries on the Macie findings that describe the log files."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Partition projection lets Athena calculate partition values from a configured pattern, so new days of logs are queryable without adding partitions. Filtering on the projected columns makes each query read only the matching account, Region, and date prefixes.",
      "Loading 3 years of logs into OpenSearch every night is a costly pipeline that the engineer would have to run.",
      "Without partitions, every query scans all of the data, which is slow and costs more.",
      "Macie findings describe sensitive data in objects. They are not the CloudTrail events."] },

{ id: "d1-28", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security operations team needs near real-time search, correlation, and dashboards across logs from AWS WAF, VPC Flow Logs, and its applications. The team wants a managed service and must be able to build correlation searches across all sources.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Stream the logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service. Build the correlation searches and dashboards in OpenSearch Dashboards.",
      "Store the logs in Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval. Restore the files and search them each time an incident occurs.",
      "Send each log source to a separate Amazon SNS topic. Have the analysts subscribe to the topics and review the messages.",
      "Enable AWS Config recording for all resources. Use AWS Config advanced queries to correlate the entries across the logs."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["OpenSearch Service is a managed search and analytics service that indexes log data in near real time. Analysts can search and correlate events across sources and build dashboards in OpenSearch Dashboards.",
      "Archived logs must be restored before they can be searched, which takes hours and is not near real time.",
      "SNS delivers messages, but it does not store, search, or correlate them.",
      "AWS Config advanced queries search resource configuration data, not log entries."] },

{ id: "d1-29", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses Amazon Security Lake. The company also runs a third-party firewall that writes logs in its own JSON format. The security team must query the firewall logs together with the AWS log sources in Security Lake by using the same schema.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create a custom source in Security Lake for the firewall, with an IAM role that the source uses to write data.",
      "Use an AWS Lambda function or AWS Glue job to convert the logs to OCSF in Apache Parquet before writing them to the custom source location.",
      "Send the firewall logs directly to the Security Lake S3 bucket in their original JSON format for each event.",
      "Create an organization trail in AWS CloudTrail that records the firewall logs as data events.",
      "Enable AWS Security Hub CSPM in the account, and import the firewall logs as Security Hub findings."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Security Lake accepts data from third-party sources through custom sources. Each custom source has a location in the Security Lake bucket and an IAM role that the source uses to write to it.",
      "Data from a custom source must already be in the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF) and stored as Apache Parquet. A conversion step makes the firewall logs match the schema of the AWS sources.",
      "Raw JSON does not match the OCSF schema, so the logs could not be queried together with the other sources.",
      "CloudTrail records AWS API activity. It cannot ingest logs from a third-party firewall.",
      "Security Hub findings are a different format and purpose, and they would not put the logs in Security Lake."] },

{ id: "d1-30", d: 1,
  q: "A company's security team wants shared dashboards that show Amazon CloudWatch metrics and Amazon Athena query results over Amazon Security Lake data. Team members must sign in through AWS IAM Identity Center. The team does not want to manage dashboard servers.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon Managed Grafana workspace that uses IAM Identity Center for sign-in. Add CloudWatch and Athena as data sources.",
      "Install Grafana on an Amazon EC2 instance in the security account. Create a local Grafana user for each member of the security team.",
      "Create a CloudWatch dashboard. Add widgets that run Athena queries and show the query results directly on the dashboard each hour.",
      "Use Amazon QuickSight with a separate IAM user for each team member. Connect QuickSight to the CloudWatch metrics as a data source."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Amazon Managed Grafana is a managed Grafana service, so there are no servers to run. It supports IAM Identity Center for sign-in and includes data sources for CloudWatch and Athena.",
      "A self-managed Grafana server must be patched and maintained, and local users bypass IAM Identity Center.",
      "CloudWatch dashboards do not run Athena queries as widgets.",
      "IAM users for each person go against the requirement to sign in through IAM Identity Center."] },

{ id: "d1-31", d: 1,
  q: "A company's applications write logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs in different formats. The company's security team needs each log event converted to a consistent JSON structure, with sensitive fields removed, and stored in Amazon S3 within minutes.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a subscription filter that sends the log events to an AWS Lambda function. Have the function convert each event, remove the sensitive fields, and write it to Amazon S3.",
      "Export the log groups to Amazon S3 each day. Ask the analysts to convert the records to the new structure when they query the exported files.",
      "Create a metric filter for each log format. Store the metric values in Amazon S3 in a consistent structure for each of the applications.",
      "Configure S3 Object Lambda on the bucket to convert the exported records each time an analyst reads them from the bucket later."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A subscription filter delivers log events in near real time to a Lambda function. The function can parse each event into one structure, remove sensitive fields, and store the result in S3.",
      "Daily exports are not within minutes, and the sensitive fields would be stored before anyone removed them.",
      "Metric filters produce numbers, not the log events themselves.",
      "Object Lambda changes data when it is read, so the stored objects would still contain the sensitive fields."] },

{ id: "d1-32", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses Amazon Security Lake. A third-party SIEM tool must receive new Security Lake data as soon as it arrives and copy the objects into its own storage. A separate analytics team must run SQL queries on the data in place without copying it.\n\nWhich subscriber configuration will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a data access subscriber for the SIEM tool. Create a query access subscriber for the analytics team.",
      "Create a query access subscriber for the SIEM tool. Create a data access subscriber for the analytics team.",
      "Create one data access subscriber for both. Share the Security Lake S3 bucket with the analytics team's account.",
      "Create one query access subscriber for both. Have the SIEM tool export the query results from Amazon Athena."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A data access subscriber is notified of new objects and reads them directly from the S3 bucket, which suits a tool that copies data. A query access subscriber queries the data in place through AWS Lake Formation and services such as Athena.",
      "This reverses the two subscriber types, so neither consumer gets the access that it needs.",
      "Sharing the bucket outside the subscriber model gives the analytics team broader access than required and does not give them query access.",
      "Exporting query results is not receiving new data as it arrives, and it adds custom work for the SIEM tool."] },

{ id: "d1-33", d: 1,
  q: "A company exposes a REST API through Amazon API Gateway. The company's security team must record the source IP address, caller identity, request path, and status code of every request to the API in Amazon CloudWatch Logs.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Enable access logging for the API stage. Define a log format that includes $context.identity.sourceIp, the caller identity, the path, and the status.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail in the account. Search the trail for the requests that clients send to the API stage, and record their source IP addresses.",
      "Enable AWS X-Ray tracing for the API stage. Review the trace for each request in the X-Ray console to find the caller and the status code.",
      "Enable detailed CloudWatch metrics for the API stage. Review the 4XXError and Count metrics for each method and resource in the API."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Access logging writes one entry for each request to CloudWatch Logs, in a format that the team defines with $context variables such as the source IP address, the caller identity, the path, and the status code.",
      "CloudTrail records calls to the API Gateway management API, not requests that clients send to the deployed API.",
      "Traces help with latency analysis, and they are sampled and not a complete log of callers.",
      "Metrics count requests and errors. They do not record who made each request."] },

{ id: "d1-34", d: 1,
  q: "A company serves a web application through Amazon CloudFront. The company's security team must analyze the client IP address, URI, user agent, and edge result of every viewer request to investigate scraping.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable CloudFront standard logging for the distribution. Deliver the access logs to Amazon S3 or Amazon CloudWatch Logs.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail for the account. Search the trail for each viewer request that reaches the CloudFront distribution.",
      "Create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm on the distribution's Requests metric. Analyze the alarm history for scraping patterns.",
      "Enable server access logging on the origin's Amazon S3 bucket. Use those logs to see every request that CloudFront serves."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront standard logs record every viewer request with fields such as the client IP address, the URI, the user agent, and the edge result type. They can be delivered to S3, CloudWatch Logs, or Amazon Data Firehose.",
      "CloudTrail records CloudFront management API calls, not viewer requests.",
      "The Requests metric counts requests. It has no per-request details.",
      "The origin sees only cache misses, so requests that CloudFront serves from cache would be missing."] },

{ id: "d1-35", d: 1,
  q: "A company stores AWS CloudTrail logs and VPC Flow Logs in an Amazon S3 bucket in a log archive account. A regulation requires that no one, including administrators in the log archive account, can delete or overwrite log files for 1 year. After 1 year, the logs must be deleted automatically.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Enable S3 Object Lock on the bucket with a default retention period of 1 year in compliance mode.",
      "Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that expires objects after 1 year and permanently deletes noncurrent versions.",
      "Enable S3 Object Lock on the bucket with a default retention period of 1 year in governance mode.",
      "Enable MFA delete on the bucket, and give the MFA device to a member of the company's security team.",
      "Add a bucket policy that denies s3:DeleteObject to every principal except the root user of the account."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["In compliance mode, no user can overwrite or delete a protected object version, or shorten its retention, until the retention period ends, and that includes the root user.",
      "After the retention period ends, a lifecycle rule removes the logs automatically. Because Object Lock uses versioning, the rule must also delete noncurrent versions to remove the data.",
      "In governance mode, users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission can delete protected objects, so administrators could still remove logs.",
      "MFA delete protects versions only from users who lack the device, and the root user can still delete them.",
      "The root user remains able to delete objects, and a bucket policy can be changed by an administrator."] },

{ id: "d1-36", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer creates an AWS CloudTrail trail that delivers to a new Amazon S3 bucket in a different account. The trail shows a delivery error, and no log files appear in the bucket. The bucket uses SSE-S3 encryption.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The bucket policy does not allow the cloudtrail.amazonaws.com service principal to call s3:PutObject on the trail's log path.",
      "The bucket does not have S3 Versioning enabled, which CloudTrail requires before it can deliver log files to any S3 bucket.",
      "The trail does not have log file validation enabled, which CloudTrail requires for delivery to a bucket in another account.",
      "The bucket uses SSE-S3 encryption, which CloudTrail does not support for the log files that it delivers to Amazon S3."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail writes log files as the cloudtrail.amazonaws.com service principal. The bucket policy must allow that principal to check the bucket ACL and to put objects in the AWSLogs path for the account, usually with an aws:SourceArn condition for the trail.",
      "Versioning is recommended for protection, but CloudTrail does not require it to deliver logs.",
      "Log file validation adds digest files. It is not required for delivery to another account.",
      "SSE-S3 is the default encryption for CloudTrail log files, so it does not block delivery."] },

{ id: "d1-37", d: 1,
  q: "A company runs Amazon EC2 instances in private subnets that have no internet access. The Amazon CloudWatch agent is installed, and the instance profile includes the CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy managed policy. No logs from the instances appear in CloudWatch Logs, and the agent log shows connection timeouts.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Create an interface VPC endpoint for CloudWatch Logs. Allow inbound HTTPS from the instances in the endpoint's security group.",
      "Attach the AdministratorAccess policy to the instance profile. Restart the CloudWatch agent on each of the instances in the subnets.",
      "Create a gateway VPC endpoint for CloudWatch Logs. Add the gateway endpoint to the route tables of each of the private subnets.",
      "Enable detailed monitoring on the instances. Wait for the agent to retry its connection to CloudWatch Logs during the next hour."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Timeouts mean the agent cannot reach the CloudWatch Logs endpoint. An interface VPC endpoint gives the private subnets a private path to the service, and its security group must allow HTTPS from the instances.",
      "The permissions are already in place, and broader permissions do not fix a network timeout.",
      "Gateway endpoints exist only for Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB, so this endpoint cannot be created.",
      "Detailed monitoring changes the EC2 metric interval. It does not create a network path for the agent."] },

{ id: "d1-38", d: 1,
  q: "A company's AWS Lambda function runs without errors, but no log group or log events appear in Amazon CloudWatch Logs for the function. The function uses a custom execution role that the security team wrote by following least privilege.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The execution role does not allow logs:CreateLogGroup, logs:CreateLogStream, and logs:PutLogEvents for the function's log group.",
      "The function's resource-based policy does not allow CloudWatch Logs to invoke the function and read the function's output.",
      "The function does not have AWS X-Ray active tracing enabled, which Lambda requires before it can write any function logs.",
      "The account has not turned on CloudWatch Logs for Lambda in the Lambda console, which is turned off by default for new accounts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Lambda sends function logs to CloudWatch Logs by using the function's execution role. Without the CloudWatch Logs permissions, such as those in the AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole managed policy, no log group or events are created.",
      "Resource-based policies control who can invoke the function. CloudWatch Logs does not invoke the function to collect logs.",
      "Tracing is separate from logging and is not required for logs.",
      "There is no account-level switch for Lambda logging. Permissions in the execution role control it."] },

{ id: "d1-39", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer enables execution logging for a REST API stage in Amazon API Gateway. When the engineer saves the setting, API Gateway returns an error that says that a CloudWatch Logs role ARN must be set in the account settings.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Create an IAM role that API Gateway can assume, with permission to write to CloudWatch Logs. Set the role's ARN in the API Gateway account settings.",
      "Add a resource policy to the API that allows the logs.amazonaws.com service principal to invoke the stage and write the execution logs for it.",
      "Create the CloudWatch Logs log group for the stage manually. Grant each of the API's users permission to write log events to the log group.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for API Gateway in the account. Execution logging then uses the trail to deliver the stage's log events."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["API Gateway writes execution and access logs to CloudWatch Logs by assuming one IAM role per Region that is set in the account settings. The role must trust apigateway.amazonaws.com and have CloudWatch Logs permissions, such as the AmazonAPIGatewayPushToCloudWatchLogs managed policy.",
      "A resource policy controls who can call the API. It does not give API Gateway permission to write logs.",
      "API Gateway writes the logs itself, not the API's users, and it still needs the account-level role.",
      "Execution logging does not use CloudTrail."] },

{ id: "d1-40", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer enables access logs for an Application Load Balancer and selects an existing Amazon S3 bucket. The load balancer reports an access denied error for the bucket, and no logs are delivered. The bucket uses SSE-S3 encryption.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The bucket policy does not grant the Elastic Load Balancing log delivery principal permission to write objects to the bucket.",
      "The bucket must use SSE-KMS with a customer managed key before Elastic Load Balancing can deliver its access logs to the bucket.",
      "The load balancer's security group does not allow outbound HTTPS traffic to the Amazon S3 service endpoint in the Region.",
      "Access logs can be delivered only to a bucket that is in a different AWS Region from the load balancer, for durability reasons."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Elastic Load Balancing writes access logs as a log delivery principal for the Region. The bucket policy must allow that principal to put objects in the log prefix, or delivery fails with access denied.",
      "Access logs support SSE-S3 for the destination bucket, so SSE-S3 is not the cause.",
      "Log delivery is done by the service, not through the load balancer's network interfaces or security group.",
      "The bucket must be in the same Region as the load balancer, not a different one."] },

{ id: "d1-41", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer creates a VPC flow log that publishes to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. The flow log shows an access error, and no log streams are created. The engineer selected an IAM role whose permissions policy allows the required CloudWatch Logs actions.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The role's trust policy does not allow the vpc-flow-logs.amazonaws.com service principal to assume the role.",
      "The VPC does not have an internet gateway, which flow logs require to deliver records to CloudWatch Logs.",
      "The log group uses the default encryption, which VPC Flow Logs does not support as a destination setting.",
      "Flow logs can publish to CloudWatch Logs only when the VPC has an interface VPC endpoint for CloudWatch Logs."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["VPC Flow Logs publishes to CloudWatch Logs by assuming the IAM role that the flow log names. The role's trust policy must allow the vpc-flow-logs.amazonaws.com service principal, or the service cannot use the permissions in the role.",
      "Flow log delivery is done by the service and does not depend on gateways in the VPC.",
      "Default encryption of the log group does not block flow log delivery.",
      "No VPC endpoint is required, because the service delivers flow logs outside the VPC's network path."] },

{ id: "d1-42", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer creates an Amazon EventBridge rule to start an AWS Lambda function for every new Amazon GuardDuty finding. GuardDuty generates findings, but the rule's MatchedEvents metric stays at 0. The rule's event pattern matches the source guardduty.amazonaws.com.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Change the event pattern to match the source aws.guardduty and the detail-type GuardDuty Finding.",
      "Add a resource-based policy to the Lambda function that allows GuardDuty to invoke the function directly.",
      "Enable GuardDuty findings export to Amazon S3, which GuardDuty requires before it sends findings to EventBridge.",
      "Move the rule to a custom event bus, because GuardDuty sends its findings to custom event buses only."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Events from AWS services use a source value of aws.<service>. GuardDuty findings arrive on the default event bus with the source aws.guardduty and the detail-type GuardDuty Finding, so the current pattern never matches.",
      "The rule never matches, so the Lambda function permissions are not the problem yet, and GuardDuty does not invoke the function directly.",
      "Exporting to S3 is a separate feature. GuardDuty sends findings to EventBridge without it.",
      "GuardDuty sends findings to the default event bus, not to custom buses."] },

{ id: "d1-43", d: 1,
  q: "An Amazon EventBridge rule sends AWS Security Hub CSPM findings to an Amazon SNS topic. After the security team enables server-side encryption on the topic with a customer managed AWS KMS key, the rule's FailedInvocations metric increases and no notifications arrive.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Update the key policy to allow the events.amazonaws.com service principal to use kms:GenerateDataKey and kms:Decrypt with the key.",
      "Replace the customer managed key with the AWS managed key for Amazon SNS, which EventBridge can always use to publish messages.",
      "Add an Amazon SQS dead-letter queue to the SNS topic. Messages that failed to publish are then delivered from the queue later on.",
      "Grant the Security Hub CSPM service principal permission to subscribe to the SNS topic in the topic's access policy as well."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["To publish to an encrypted topic, EventBridge must be able to use the KMS key. The key policy must allow the events.amazonaws.com service principal to call kms:GenerateDataKey and kms:Decrypt.",
      "The AWS managed key's policy cannot be changed to allow EventBridge, so publishing would still fail.",
      "A dead-letter queue captures failed deliveries, but it does not fix the missing key permission.",
      "Security Hub does not publish to the topic. EventBridge does, and the subscription permission does not affect encryption."] },

{ id: "d1-44", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses AWS Security Hub CSPM and Amazon GuardDuty in us-east-1 and eu-west-1. The security team reviews findings in Security Hub CSPM in us-east-1, but GuardDuty findings from eu-west-1 never appear there.\n\nWhich combination of steps will resolve this issue? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Enable Security Hub CSPM in eu-west-1 to receive the GuardDuty findings from that Region.",
      "Configure cross-Region aggregation with us-east-1 as the home Region and eu-west-1 as a linked Region.",
      "Enable GuardDuty findings export to an Amazon S3 bucket in the us-east-1 Region for the account.",
      "Create an AWS Config aggregator in us-east-1 that collects data from eu-west-1 for the account.",
      "Enable GuardDuty in us-east-1 only, and turn off GuardDuty in eu-west-1 to send all findings there."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Security Hub CSPM is a Regional service. GuardDuty sends findings to Security Hub CSPM in the same Region, so Security Hub CSPM must be enabled in eu-west-1 to receive them.",
      "Cross-Region aggregation then copies findings from the linked Region to the home Region, so the team sees eu-west-1 findings in us-east-1.",
      "An S3 export is a separate copy of findings that Security Hub CSPM does not read.",
      "An AWS Config aggregator collects configuration data, not Security Hub findings.",
      "GuardDuty in us-east-1 cannot detect threats in eu-west-1, so this would remove protection."] },

{ id: "d1-45", d: 1,
  q: "A company enables AWS Security Hub CSPM and the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard in a new account. Many controls show no data, and their findings never update.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["AWS Config is not recording resources in the account, and most controls depend on AWS Config rules.",
      "Amazon GuardDuty is not enabled in the account, and the controls use GuardDuty to evaluate each resource.",
      "The account does not have AWS CloudTrail Lake, which Security Hub CSPM uses to store the control results.",
      "The standard takes 30 days to evaluate a new account, after which every control starts to report a status."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Security Hub CSPM runs most control checks through AWS Config rules that it creates. If AWS Config is not enabled and recording the needed resource types, those controls have no data to evaluate.",
      "GuardDuty sends threat findings to Security Hub CSPM, but controls do not depend on it.",
      "CloudTrail Lake is not used to store control results.",
      "Controls usually start reporting soon after the standard is enabled. There is no 30-day wait."] },

{ id: "d1-46", d: 1,
  q: "During an investigation, a security engineer finds that the company's AWS CloudTrail trail has no events for API calls that an attacker made in ap-southeast-2. The trail was created in us-east-1 by using an old AWS CLI script.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The trail is a single-Region trail that logs events only in us-east-1. It must be updated to log events in all Regions.",
      "CloudTrail does not log API calls in ap-southeast-2 unless Amazon GuardDuty is also enabled in that Region for the account.",
      "The trail's S3 bucket is in us-east-1, and CloudTrail cannot deliver events from other Regions to a bucket in us-east-1.",
      "The attacker used temporary credentials, and CloudTrail does not log API calls that are signed with temporary credentials."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A trail created with the AWS CLI is a single-Region trail unless it is created as a multi-Region trail. It logs events only in its home Region, so activity in ap-southeast-2 was not recorded.",
      "CloudTrail logging does not depend on GuardDuty.",
      "A multi-Region trail delivers events from every Region to one bucket, wherever the bucket is.",
      "CloudTrail logs calls that are made with temporary credentials, and it records the session details."] },

{ id: "d1-47", d: 1,
  q: "A security engineer updates the Amazon CloudWatch agent configuration in Parameter Store to collect an additional audit log file. Several days later, the instances still send only the log files from the old configuration.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Run the AmazonCloudWatch-ManageAgent document with the configure action and the Parameter Store configuration on the instances.",
      "Attach the CloudWatchAgentAdminPolicy policy to the instance profile. The agent then reloads the new configuration automatically.",
      "Delete and re-create the log groups for the instances. The agent then reads the new configuration from Parameter Store on its own.",
      "Enable detailed monitoring on the instances. The agent then checks Parameter Store for a new configuration every 5 minutes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The agent reads its configuration when it is configured and started. Running the AmazonCloudWatch-ManageAgent document with the configure action fetches the new configuration from Parameter Store and restarts the agent with it.",
      "The admin policy lets the agent write configurations to Parameter Store. It does not make running agents reload.",
      "Log groups are destinations. Re-creating them does not change what the agent collects.",
      "Detailed monitoring is unrelated to the agent's configuration."] },

{ id: "d1-48", d: 1,
  q: "Amazon Macie reports that it cannot analyze many objects in an Amazon S3 bucket. The objects are encrypted with a customer managed AWS KMS key. Macie analyzes the objects that use SSE-S3 in the same bucket without errors.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Update the key policy to allow the Macie service-linked role to use the key to decrypt the objects.",
      "Change the bucket's default encryption to SSE-S3. Macie then decrypts the existing SSE-KMS objects.",
      "Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket. Macie analyzes encrypted objects only in versioned buckets.",
      "Add a bucket policy that allows the macie.amazonaws.com principal to call s3:GetObject on all objects."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Macie uses its service-linked role to read objects. For objects encrypted with a customer managed key, the key policy must allow that role to call kms:Decrypt, or Macie cannot analyze them.",
      "Changing default encryption affects only new objects, and existing objects stay encrypted with the customer managed key.",
      "Versioning has nothing to do with whether Macie can decrypt objects.",
      "S3 access is not the problem, because Macie reads SSE-S3 objects in the same bucket. The missing permission is on the KMS key."] },

{ id: "d1-49", d: 1,
  q: "A company must be notified within minutes whenever any security group in its account allows inbound SSH from 0.0.0.0/0. Each notification must identify the security group that changed.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Enable the restricted-ssh AWS Config managed rule with configuration change triggers.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule for compliance changes of the rule that targets an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Run an Amazon Inspector scan of all of the security groups every hour, and email each scan report.",
      "Enable VPC Flow Logs for the account, and create an alarm on rejected SSH traffic to the instances.",
      "Create a weekly AWS Trusted Advisor report that lists the security groups that have open SSH ports."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["This managed rule evaluates security groups when their configuration changes and marks a group as noncompliant if it allows unrestricted incoming SSH.",
      "AWS Config sends each compliance change to EventBridge with the resource ID, so the rule can notify the team within minutes and name the security group.",
      "Amazon Inspector does not run hourly scans of security group rules or email reports.",
      "Rejected traffic does not show that a rule now allows SSH from anywhere.",
      "A weekly report is not within minutes."] },

{ id: "d1-50", d: 1,
  q: "A company has an Amazon GuardDuty delegated administrator account for its organization. The security team must send every new GuardDuty finding from all member accounts to an external SIEM tool that accepts events at an HTTPS endpoint. The solution must not require resources in each member account.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an Amazon EventBridge rule in the delegated administrator account that matches GuardDuty findings.",
      "Set an EventBridge API destination for the SIEM tool's HTTPS endpoint as the target of the rule.",
      "Create an EventBridge rule in each member account that sends its findings to the SIEM endpoint.",
      "Export the GuardDuty findings from each member account to an Amazon S3 bucket in that account.",
      "Enable GuardDuty checks in AWS Trusted Advisor, and connect Trusted Advisor to the SIEM tool."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["GuardDuty publishes findings for member accounts to EventBridge in the administrator account as well, so one rule in that account matches findings from the whole organization.",
      "An API destination sends matched events to an HTTPS endpoint, with authentication and retry handling, so no custom code is needed.",
      "Rules in every member account are the per-account resources that the company wants to avoid.",
      "Exports in each member account are per-account resources, and the SIEM tool would have to read many buckets.",
      "Trusted Advisor does not forward GuardDuty findings to external tools."] },
]);
